<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#blue-riband">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Blue Riband</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Blue Riband is an unofficial honour given to the passenger liner making the fastest regular crossing of the Atlantic. Held by British ships for much of the 19th century, it was won by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norddeutscher-lloyd">Norddeutscher Lloyd</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ss-kaiser-wilhelm-der-grosse">Kaiser Wilhelm der Grosse</a> in 1897 and by its liner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> in 1929. The contest drove rivalry between British, German, Italian and American shipping lines.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/blue-riband.jpg" alt="Blue Riband" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_169487_United_States_(ship).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Blue Riband is an unofficial accolade given to the passenger liner in regular service crossing the Atlantic with the highest average speed. The term was borrowed from horse racing and was not widely used until after 1910. The record rests on average speed rather than passage time because ships follow different routes, and eastbound and westbound records are reckoned separately, since the westbound crossing, against the Gulf Stream and the prevailing weather, typically gives lower averages. Of the 35 liners that held it, 25 were British, five German, three American and one each Italian and French. No formal award existed until 1935, when Harold K. Hales donated the Hales Trophy, whose rules differed from the traditional ones.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before steam, eastbound passages of 30 to 45 days were not uncommon, and westward passages of 65 to 90 days excited no attention. The contest is traced back to 1838, when the steamships Sirius and Great Western, the first to offer regular scheduled service, raced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Sirius, chartered by the British and American Steam Navigation Company, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a> four days before Great Western left Avonmouth and had to burn spars when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> ran low. Her westbound crossing at 8.03 knots made her often considered the first record holder, though Great Western arrived faster. Cunard officially refused to recognise the title because racing did not suit its safety policy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Cunard began service in 1840 and survived thanks to an Admiralty mail subsidy. The Collins Line, subsidised by the American government, set a record in 1850 with the Pacific, but it collapsed in 1858 after losing two steamers. Cunard's Scotia in 1862 was the last paddle steamer to set a record. Later the record passed among White Star, Inman, Guion, German lines such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norddeutscher-lloyd">Norddeutscher Lloyd</a> and Hamburg-America, and Cunard's Lusitania and Mauretania. Mauretania's record of 26.06 knots from 1909 stood for 20 years, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> took it in 1929 at 27.83 knots. Many liners were built with government <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> and with military use in mind; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> estimated that the two Cunard Queens shortened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> by a year.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> set the record in 1952 at 35.59 knots eastbound and 34.51 westbound, and no passenger liner has broken it. Jet airliners entered service in 1958, ending the era of the record-breakers. In 1986 Richard Branson's Virgin Atlantic Challenger beat the speed but was not a passenger vessel, and in 1990 the Incat catamaran Hoverspeed Great Britain took the Hales Trophy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Blue Riband” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Bremen in 1857, made the city one of the great emigration ports of Europe and a link between Germany and the United States; Lloyd liners such as the Kaiser Wilhelm der Grosse of 1897 and the Bremen of 1929 won the <b>Blue Riband</b> for the fastest Atlantic crossing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Free Hanseatic City of Bremen · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
